FitCoach Platform — Data Model & API Documentation (v3)
Backend: Supabase (Postgres + Auth + Storage + Realtime + Edge Functions) Revision history:

v2 — architecture review: template/assignment split, snapshot-based versioning, organization_members join table, explicit RLS patterns, isolated wearable credentials, real messaging tables, media as FK references. Deliberately deferred: full version-history tables, full session/set-log granularity — see §8.
v3 — adds habit tracking (§9), coach/gym seat licensing and the invite-based connection flow (§10), and the paid Programs marketplace with trainer payouts (§11). See §14 for the consolidated list of business and technical decisions still open.
1. System Overview
Three coaching modes share one model, differentiated by visibility on workout_cards, org_id, and type on coaching_relationships. A fourth concept — assignment — now sits between a card and a client, whether the coaching is trainer-driven or the client self-started a card from Discover:

Mode	Relationship	Card visibility	Assignment source
Private coaching	trainer ↔ client, no org	private	trainer_assigned
Gym coaching	trainer ↔ client, shared org_id	gym_only	trainer_assigned
Public library, self-directed	none required	public	self_saved
A client can hold all three at once. Logging always happens against an assignment, never against the template directly — that's what keeps Progress unified regardless of how the workout got into the client's hands, and what stops a trainer's later edit to a template from rewriting a client's history.

