4. Row Level Security — patterns, not just intent
RLS in Postgres does not auto-inherit through a foreign key — each table needs its own policy, typically checking the parent via EXISTS.

workout_cards (SELECT):

visibility = 'public'
OR trainer_id = auth.uid()
OR (visibility = 'gym_only' AND EXISTS (
      SELECT 1 FROM organization_members om
      WHERE om.org_id = workout_cards.org_id AND om.user_id = auth.uid() AND om.status = 'active'
    ))
INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE: trainer_id = auth.uid() only.

exercises (SELECT) — inherits by checking the parent explicitly:

EXISTS (
  SELECT 1 FROM workout_cards wc
  WHERE wc.id = exercises.card_id
    AND ( wc.visibility = 'public'
          OR wc.trainer_id = auth.uid()
          OR EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM organization_members om
                     WHERE om.org_id = wc.org_id AND om.user_id = auth.uid() AND om.status = 'active') )
)
assignment_exercises / workout_logs follow the same shape, checking up through workout_assignments.client_id = auth.uid() OR workout_assignments.trainer_id = auth.uid().

wearable_metrics / wearable_connections: client_id = auth.uid() only — never exposed to trainers directly (see the trainer-client-dashboard Edge Function in §6.6, which returns an aggregated view instead).

organization_members: readable by any member of the same org_id; writable only by rows where the caller's own membership role is owner or admin.

messages / conversation_members: readable/writable only where auth.uid() has a row in conversation_members for that conversation_id.

