11.5 Free vs. paid — a framework
Free: trainer profile, certifications/verified badge, 1–2 public cards, a sampler habit or two — enough to build trust and get discovered.
Paid: full multi-week structured programs, habit bundles tied to a program, video-demo-rich content.
You can also tie publishing rights to the seat plan tier (§10) rather than gating purely on content: e.g. Starter = roster tool only, Growth = can publish free public content, Pro = can publish paid Programs at a lower platform fee %. Gives an upsell path beyond raw seat count.

11.6 Platform fee model — yes, this works
Confirming the mechanic you proposed: platform collects the full client payment, deducts a platform fee %, and pays the remainder to the coach. This is the standard Patreon/Udemy/Gumroad marketplace pattern, not something unusual to build.

Platform fee: typical marketplace range 15–25% — open decision, see §14.
Payout cadence: monthly is the simpler default for reconciliation load. 15-day is more coach-friendly but roughly doubles payout processing — a common middle ground is monthly payouts with a minimum threshold (e.g. hold and roll over anything under ₹500).
11.7 RLS — gating paid content
A paid program's card still needs to be visible in Discover as a listing (title, cover, price) even to non-subscribers, but its exercises should only be readable by someone with access:

-- exercises SELECT, extended for paid gating
EXISTS (
  SELECT 1 FROM workout_cards wc
  WHERE wc.id = exercises.card_id
    AND ( wc.trainer_id = auth.uid()
          OR NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM programs p WHERE p.workout_card_id = wc.id AND p.price_inr > 0)
          OR EXISTS (
               SELECT 1 FROM programs p
               JOIN program_subscriptions ps ON ps.program_id = p.id
               WHERE p.workout_card_id = wc.id
                 AND ps.client_id = auth.uid()
                 AND ps.status = 'active'
             ) )
)
Same pattern extends to program_habits → habit_templates.

11.8 Compliance note — do not skip this
Splitting a client's payment between the platform and multiple coaches falls under RBI's Payment Aggregator regulations in India — this needs a payment aggregator built for marketplace/split settlement (Razorpay Route, Cashfree Easy Split) rather than custom payout logic, plus GST and TDS handling (Section 194-O applies to e-commerce operators paying sellers). This is a genuine "talk to a CA or fintech consultant before real money moves" item, not something to spec purely at the schema level.

